<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Putna Monastery</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1466">1466</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Putna Monastery is a Romanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a>, north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-the-great">Stephen the Great</a> in 1466 and consecrated in 1470. It became the burial place of Stephen and his family and a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> copying and embroidery. Rebuilt after damage in the 17th century, it remains a place of national pilgrimage in Romania.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/putna-monastery.jpg" alt="Putna Monastery" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Geboiu Dumitru Mihai · CC BY-SA 3.0 ro · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gr%C4%83dina_Edenului_numit%C4%83_Putna_impresionat%C4%83_de_astrul_de_foc.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Orthodox Church, using Church Slavonic in its liturgy and chancery until the seventeenth century, was the main patron of culture, and monasteries such as <b>Putna</b>, Neamț and Sucevița were centres of learning, manuscript copying and embroidery.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
